    "cooling-tech.html": """<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Thermodynamics of Closed-Loop Cooling: Direct-to-Chip & Dry Coolers | GreenCompute</title>
  <meta name="description" content="Engineering analysis of closed-circuit liquid cooling, dry coolers, technology cooling systems, and interactive PUE/WUE calculators.">
  <link rel="canonical" href="https://sustainable-dc.pages.dev/cooling-tech.html">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>

  <header class="masthead">
    <div class="container masthead-inner">
      <a href="index.html" class="brand">GreenCompute DB <span class="brand-tag">Research Index</span></a>
      <nav class="main-nav">
        <ul>
          <li><a href="facilities.html">Facilities</a></li>
          <li><a href="cooling-tech.html" class="active">Thermodynamics</a></li>
          <li><a href="regulations.html">Statutory Codes</a></li>
          <li><a href="baseload-nuclear.html">Clean Baseload</a></li>
          <li><a href="sources.html">Source Index</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="container">
    <section style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <span class="badge badge-blue">Thermal Engineering Analysis</span>
      <h1>Thermodynamic Mechanics of Closed-Loop Cooling</h1>
      <p class="lead">Cooling systems historically represented up to 40% of total data center electrical demand. As rack densities exceed 50 to 100 kW, traditional air cooling and evaporative towers reach physical limits.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Verified Exhibit: Server Aisle -->
    <figure class="media-figure">
      <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Energy_Efficient_Processors.jpg/1280px-Energy_Efficient_Processors.jpg" alt="High-density energy efficient compute server racks" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption class="media-caption">
        <strong>Exhibit 3:</strong> High-density compute racks. Liquid cold plates remove heat directly from the silicon die, allowing rack power densities to exceed 100 kW without consuming water. Photo by Intel Free Press via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Energy_Efficient_Processors.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.0)</a>.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The Water Consumption Baseline</h2>
    <p>Facility efficiency is quantified using standardized thermodynamic and resource metrics:</p>

    <div class="statute-quote">
      $$\\text{PUE} = \\frac{\\text{Total Facility Energy Consumption}}{\\text{IT Equipment Energy Consumption}}$$
      <br>
      $$\\text{WUE} = \\frac{\\text{Annual Water Consumption (Liters)}}{\\text{IT Equipment Energy Consumption (kWh)}}$$
      <span class="statute-cite">The Green Grid / ASHRAE TC 9.9 Efficiency Standard</span>
    </div>

    <p>Traditional data centers rely on open-loop evaporative cooling towers. Evaporation dissipates heat via the latent heat of vaporization, consuming between 1.5 and 3.0 Liters per kWh, yielding an industry-average WUE of 1.8 L/kWh. Under peak summer thermal conditions, a large hyperscale facility running open-loop towers consumes between 1 and 5 million gallons of potable water daily—an extraction rate comparable to a city of 30,000 to 50,000 residents.</p>
    <p>Approximately 70% to 80% of this water is lost to the atmosphere through evaporation, while the remaining 20% to 30% is discharged into municipal sewers as mineral-dense blowdown wastewater. Furthermore, off-site thermoelectric and hydroelectric generation consumes an additional 7.6 Liters/kWh across the utility supply chain.</p>

    <h2>Closed-Loop Direct-to-Chip (DLC) Mechanics</h2>
    <p>To eliminate on-site water consumption, modern architectures deploy closed-loop Direct-to-Chip liquid cooling paired with exterior closed-circuit dry coolers. In this system:</p>
    <ol style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <li><strong>Primary Loop:</strong> A sealed primary loop circulates treated dielectric fluid or water through micro-channel cold plates mounted directly against GPUs and CPUs. Heat transfers conductively from silicon into the fluid.</li>
      <li><strong>Technology Cooling System (TCS):</strong> The hot primary fluid passes through a Technology Cooling System heat exchanger, transferring thermal energy to an isolated secondary loop.</li>
      <li><strong>External Dry Coolers:</strong> The secondary loop pumps the rejected heat to rooftop or exterior dry coolers equipped with high-efficiency variable-speed fan banks, shedding heat directly into ambient air without any fluid contact with the atmosphere.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Once charged with fluid during commissioning, the closed system operates with zero evaporative water consumption, achieving a verified operational WUE of 0.0 L/kWh and eliminating potable water reliance.</p>

    <!-- Interactive Modeling Tool -->
    <section class="tool-box">
      <h3>Interactive Water-Energy Impact Calculator</h3>
      <p>Model the potable water saved annually by converting a hyperscale AI compute campus from open-loop evaporative towers to a closed-loop dry cooler system.</p>

      <div class="slider-unit">
        <label for="calc-mw">
          <span>Campus IT Electrical Demand (MW):</span>
          <span id="mw-val">50 MW</span>
        </label>
        <input type="range" id="calc-mw" min="10" max="500" value="50" step="5">
      </div>

      <div class="slider-unit">
        <label for="calc-wue">
          <span>Evaporative Cooling Tower Baseline WUE (L/kWh):</span>
          <span id="wue-val">1.8 L/kWh</span>
        </label>
        <input type="range" id="calc-wue" min="1.0" max="3.0" value="1.8" step="0.1">
      </div>

      <div class="tool-metrics">
        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-num" id="metric-evap">788.4M</span>
          <span class="metric-desc">Annual Evaporative Water Loss (Liters)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-num" id="metric-closed">0 L</span>
          <span class="metric-desc">Closed-Loop Operational Water</span>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-num" id="metric-saved" style="color: var(--primary);">788.4M L</span>
          <span class="metric-desc">Potable Water Conserved Annually</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <h2>The Latent Heat Trade-Off: Elevated Operating Temperatures</h2>
    <p>While closed-circuit dry coolers eliminate water consumption, rejecting heat into warm ambient air requires higher fan and pumping power, potentially increasing facility PUE. To manage this trade-off without energy penalties, operators elevate server inlet operating fluid temperatures above 27°C (80.6°F) up to 45°C (113°F). This widened delta-T between the server fluid and ambient outdoor air enables passive economization even in hot climates, allowing facilities to maintain low PUE (< 1.20) while maintaining zero water consumption.</p>
