    <h2>1. Germany: The Energy Efficiency Act (EnEfG § 11)</h2>
    <p>Germany's <em>Energieeffizienzgesetz</em> (EnEfG) represents Europe's strictest regulatory compliance framework for compute infrastructure:</p>
    <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <li><strong>PUE Thresholds:</strong> Facilities commencing operation on or after July 1, 2026, must achieve an annual average PUE of &le; 1.2. Existing assets must achieve &le; 1.5 by July 1, 2027, and &le; 1.3 by July 1, 2030.</li>
      <li><strong>Mandatory Waste Heat Reuse (<em>Abwärmenutzung</em>):</strong> Section 11 establishes mandatory energy reuse quotas: new data centers must export at least 10% of their waste heat starting July 1, 2026, 15% starting July 1, 2027, and 20% starting July 1, 2028.</li>
      <li><strong>Renewable Electricity Procurement:</strong> Operators were legally required to cover 50% of electrical consumption with verified renewable electricity by January 1, 2024, scaling to 100% by January 1, 2027.</li>
      <li><strong>Inlet Operating Temperature:</strong> Section 11(6) mandates that air-cooled facilities maintain server inlet temperatures of at least 27°C (80.6°F) to elevate return thermal grades, making the waste heat commercially viable for municipal district heating grids (<em>Fernwärmenetze</em>).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Ireland: Commission for Regulation of Utilities (CRU)</h2>
    <p>Facing extreme transmission grid congestion from data centers (which consume over 20% of Ireland's total electricity), the CRU implemented strict connection criteria for Large Energy Users:</p>
    <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <li><strong>80% Renewable Additionality:</strong> Developers cannot simply purchase existing grid renewable credits. They must demonstrate that at least 80% of their annual electrical demand is covered by newly developed, additional renewable energy projects within Ireland.</li>
      <li><strong>100% Dispatchable Backup:</strong> Data centers must install on-site dispatchable generation or Battery Energy Storage Systems (BESS) capable of supplying 100% of their Maximum Import Capacity (MIC).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Singapore: DC-CFA2 and the Green Data Centre Roadmap</h2>
    <p>Following the lifting of its 2019 data center moratorium, the Infocomm Media Development Authority (IMDA) and Economic Development Board (EDB) instituted the DC-CFA2 framework:</p>
    <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <li><strong>PUE Cap of 1.25:</strong> All new data centers must meet the BCA-IMDA Green Mark for Data Centres Platinum tier and guarantee an operational PUE of &le; 1.25 at full load—the strictest standard in a tropical climate worldwide.</li>
      <li><strong>Green Power Sourcing:</strong> Operators must source at least 50% of their power from green pathways (direct solar, low-carbon hydrogen, or cross-border clean electricity imports).</li>
    </ul>
